<resources>
    <!--
      Window theme for the Compose UI.

      This file used to pin windowBackground, statusBarColor, navigationBarColor
      and every surface to opaque black, left over from the View-based UI. Two
      consequences: the status bar was never transparent (it was a solid black
      rectangle drawn by the system, so content could not run under it), and the
      app ignored the device light/dark setting because the window colors were
      hardcoded.

      The colours now come from Compose (MiuixTheme via DfTheme) and the window
      itself is transparent, so the app follows the system scheme and draws edge
      to edge. DayNight in the parent keeps the pre-Compose starting window and
      the system splash consistent with the chosen scheme.

      The popupMenuStyle / actionOverflowMenuStyle / ToolbarTitle / DF_MenuText
      entries are gone: they styled android.widget popups and a Toolbar that no
      longer exist now that the UI is Compose.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.DirtyFrag" parent="Theme.Material3.DynamicColors.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:fontFamily">@font/inter</item>
        <item name="fontFamily">@font/inter</item>
        <item name="android:letterSpacing">0</item>

        <!-- Edge to edge: the system bars draw over our content and the app is
             responsible for insetting itself (Scaffold and SmallTopAppBar
             handle that). Transparent bars are what make that work. -->
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowDrawsSystemBarBackgrounds">true</item>
        <!-- No scrim behind the gesture/navigation bar. -->
        <item name="android:enforceNavigationBarContrast">false</item>
        <item name="android:enforceStatusBarContrast">false</item>
        <!-- The window background sits behind Compose. It must NOT be opaque
             black: referencing colorBackground lets the DayNight parent pick
             the right value per scheme, which is what makes the app follow the
             system theme instead of being dark-only by construction. -->
        <item name="android:windowBackground">?android:attr/colorBackground</item>

        <!-- System splash. Background follows the scheme for the same reason;
             the animated icon is transparent so no icon is shown. -->
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenBackground">?android:attr/colorBackground</item>
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@android:color/transparent</item>

        <!-- Press highlight used by any remaining platform widget. -->
        <item name="colorControlHighlight">#4D6E6E6E</item>
        <item name="android:textColorHighlight">#4D6E6E6E</item>
    </style>
</resources>
